[39.613659, "o", "I found the cause. The job silently skipped 5 of last night's 26 orders, so their quantities were \r\nnever taken out of stock. It still printed OK.\r\n\r\n**What happened**\r\n\r\n1. Five orders failed the SKU format check (`AAA-0000`) at `validate.py:24` (exceptions \r\n`r1:e1`–`r1:e5`).\r\n2. `sync_orders` catches every exception at `job.py:57`, logs a warning and moves on. Logging is \r\nset to ERROR by default, so the warning never appeared. Nothing is printed and the exit code stays \r\n0, so cron reported OK.\r\n3. The database confirms it: these five order IDs are missing from `applied_orders`, while every \r\norder around them was applied:\r\n   **SO-20521, SO-20526, SO-20530, SO-20533, SO-20537**\r\n4. Stock compared with this morning's shelf count (`data/count.csv`):\r\n\r\n   | SKU | On hand | Counted | Diff |\r\n   |---|---|---|---|\r\n   | KBD-0101 | 103 | 94 | +9 |\r\n   | HDS-0402 | 61 | 57 | +4 |\r\n   | MON-0201 | 38 | 37 | +1 |\r\n\r\n   The database shows 14 more units than the shelves across these three SKUs, which fits 5 lost \r\norders. Every other SKU matches the count.\r\n\r\n**Why the SKUs failed**\r\n\r\nThe recording keeps value sizes but not the values themselves. Two of the rejected SKUs were 9 \r\ncharacters long instead of 8, which points to extra whitespace. The other three were 8 characters \r\nbut didn't match the pattern, which points to something like lowercase letters. `parse.py:39` \r\npasses `sku=row[\"sku\"]` through unchanged, even though it strips `order_id` and `channel` and \r\nlowercases `unit`. So the likely root cause is that some SKUs from the sales API aren't cleaned up.\r\n\r\n**Why this matters more than a normal skip**\r\n\r\nThe README says the sales API hands each order over only once, because it keeps a cursor. Those 5 \r\norders won't come back in tonight's run, so the stock won't fix itself. It has to be corrected by \r\nhand: for KBD-0101, HDS-0402 and MON-0201, get the 5 orders from the sales system (or use the \r\ncount) and set the stock to match.\r\n\r\n**Suggested fixes**\r\n\r\n- In `parse.py`, clean the SKU the same way as the other fields: `sku=row[\"sku\"].strip().upper()`.\r\n- In `job.py`, stop reporting OK when orders were skipped. Count the rejected orders, log them at \r\nERROR level (cron mails those), and exit non-zero if any were rejected. Also consider keeping \r\nrejected orders somewhere they can be replayed, since the API can't resend them.\r\n\r\nI haven't changed any code or data yet. I can make the two code fixes if you want, but the stock \r\ncorrection needs the real order lines from the sales system.\r\n\r\n"]
[42.879238, "o", "\u001b[2m-- end of the run: 6 tool calls, 42 s --\u001b[0m\r\n"]
